## Abstract

The content simply talks about the sources used to train the AIs and
whether they are of ethical origin. Companies and devs have an ethical
and moral dilemma about using AI because they have no control or
traceability over where its contributions came from.

## Using LLMs to program?

[NetBSD](https://www.netbsd.org/developers/commit-guidelines.html), in its
guidelines for developers, has set the following rules:

```text
If you commit code that was not written by yourself, double check that the
license on that code permits import into the NetBSD source repository,
and permits free distribution. Check with the author(s) of the code, make
sure that they were the sole author of the code and verify with them that
they did not copy any other code.

Code generated by a large language model or similar technology,
such as GitHub/Microsoft's Copilot, OpenAI's ChatGPT, or Facebook/Meta's Code Llama,
is presumed to be tainted code, and must not be committed without prior
written approval by core.
```

Which boils down to not allowing code generated by
_Copilot_, _ChatGPT_, _Llama_, or other AIs.

They only allow code generated entirely by people.

Why?

First, the _AIs_ are trained with millions of lines of code
available on GitHub and other places on the internet.

Each piece of code can have a free or proprietary license,
which have rules that developers must comply with
and for which companies can be held legally responsible.

How do you verify that you have authorization to use the code?

If you do not have an exhaustive analysis of the licenses of each piece
of code used to train the AI and can certify that you are authorized,
it is better not to use these AIs for support.

### Steam

For example, in the case of AIs for generating artistic
content such as images, audio, and text, [Steam](http://web.archive.org/web/20240110165606/https://gamerant.com/steam-ai-games/)
initially banned them, but later allowed them on the condition
that creators declared that it was AI-generated content
and that it was free of copyright abuses.

With code it is harder to certify this, since
it is not the final product and it is difficult to identify
whether a piece of code was created by a person or an AI.

For entities like *NetBSD*, the provenance of code
and the legality of its authorship are of vital importance.

### Stolen Code

Can you ensure that the code used to train ChatGPT was not stolen or
does not have a license that forbids its use or conflicts with the project?

There have been multiple code leaks from companies like Rockstar and GTA.

```embed
https://www.xataka.com/videojuegos/codigo-fuente-completo-gta-v-filtrado-a-multiples-detalles-proyectos-cancelados-rockstar | El código fuente completo de GTA V se filtra con múltiples detalles de proyectos cancelados de Rockstar | La filtración expone el código del juego y material de proyectos cancelados del estudio.
```

How can you ensure that the stolen code was not used to train the AI?

It is ethically questionable. A gray zone that must be clarified over time.
It is very unlikely that you can detect the provenance of this code,
so it is assumed that any code created with these AIs is
contaminated and must be appropriately evaluated for whether it should
be included in the repository or not.

So *NetBSD* chose the simplest path and removed
AI-generated code from its project (unless there is
a real justification and it is authorized by those
responsible for the project).

### Samsung

So the alternatives would be two:

1. Create your own AI and train it only with code whose
origin and authorization for use in AI training you can certify.

2. Do not use AIs like Copilot and the like.

There are many companies that do not allow their developers to use
AI tools for this same reason and because
every time you use the AI you are feeding its algorithm,
which can cause company secrets to leak,
and also, being an external service, you cannot control
or delete content that has been uploaded.

As in the case of [Samsung](http://web.archive.org/web/20240518225650/https://www.forbes.com/sites/siladityaray/2023/05/02/samsung-bans-chatgpt-and-other-chatbots-for-employees-after-sensitive-code-leak/).

## Frequently Asked Questions

### Fear of progress?

No, because AIs are tools like any other.
The drama is how they were built.

It is a question of ethics.

Can you certify that they were trained without violating
the licenses?

If you build an AI whose training material you can certify,
there is no drama in using it.

### How do you certify that you did it?

How do you discriminate whether the code was created by a human or by an
LLM that a human then cleaned up? What is the practical difference between
that last case and what used to be done with Stack Overflow? What is the difference
with what we used to do when we went online, googled code,
took it and used it for our developments?

Simply, the person should use a <https://developercertificate.org/>
ethical certificate of authorship.

This is just being ethical about the creation of our projects.

It is a document that certifies that you have the rights to use the
code because you were the author. It is not that they ask for it; it is simply an example of how we can certify our authorship.
The difference between a person and an AI is that a person can be
sued, has legal responsibility. An AI cannot be held responsible
and is limited to its learning parameters.

### Why consider the ethics of using AI?

Entities like the [Free Software Foundation](https://www.fsf.org/)
have been defending people's computing rights since 1985.
Which means that the conversation about the ethics of computing is a
topic that must be considered perennial and relevant for any
member of the computing industry.

### Am I a worse dev for using AI in my code?

If you are a dev who needs these AIs constantly,
I think it is like riding a bicycle with training
wheels, wheels that can cause you an accident.

I am not saying they are bad per se; it is simply that
a kind of codependency is generated. One could
argue that syntax highlighting, autocorrect, and other
support tools are equally like crutches. But here
we are talking about code authorship and
the ethics of how that final result was reached.

There is no ethical dilemma in using the syntax highlighter,
as there is in using an LLM to generate code.

For people who already have a lot of experience
they may be a great help. Simply
use them in moderation and with consideration
for both your professional growth and the organization
for which you do the projects. Do not use them
as if they were the panacea and a silver bullet for
every problem.

### What happens in production environments?

One may think that this may be something that does not apply to production
environments, since in every system there is pre-existing
code generated by third parties.

If the templates and files have clear provenance and their license use
appropriately defined, there is no drama in using them.

On the other hand, code generated by an LLM is from a gray zone where you
do not know where it came from or what license it has.

_Steam_, for example, states that you must declare that it was AI-generated in each asset of the game.
Here you should declare that your code was AI-generated every time you use it, so that the company can decide whether or not to accept the
contribution created by AI.

## Honest Professional

In my personal opinion we must be honest
and inform the companies we do projects for
that AI tools will be used for code generation.

The company must be aware of the implications of the gray
zone and decide whether to allow them within its project or not.

As professionals we must take these
details into consideration, because they can cause
economic or intellectual losses and legal problems in the company
if the respective evaluations are not made.

Perhaps a possible help would be to put a BOT in PR generation
with a simple checkbox that must be checked, saying:

"I used AI to generate this code".

Or something similar, and that way the dev will be able to certify whether they used these tools or not.

> "The chance that an open source project will successfully sue you for a single line is zero, [but] as the size of generated code increases -- which is what developers will want -- companies must be alert," Cornwall said. "At some point, the generated code could get big enough to warrant a legal notice that your code contains IP [intellectual property] from a permissively licensed project."

## Jetbrains

Jetbrains has done something that seems very accurate to me:
having a Machine Learning model integrated into its
editors (Webstorm, IntelliJ, etc) that only runs
locally and does not send code to the cloud.

In addition, it was trained using ethically
obtained code, respecting the licenses of the creators.

> JetBrains trained its own programming language-specific, 100-million-parameter small language model using a data set of open source code with permissive licenses for this release.

> "GitHub Copilot gained so much popularity because of its integration and seamless code generation within Visual Studio," Thurai said. "However, licensing concerns and the telemetry the software sends back to Microsoft have been a concern for a lot of customers, so locally run code generation AI models became popular."

In my opinion I think it is the correct option and the appropriate
tool.

- <https://www.techtarget.com/searchsoftwarequality/news/366579752/JetBrains-IDEs-add-local-AI-code-completion>

## Conclusion

Using AI tools is ethically questionable
if you cannot certify the licenses and origin of the sources used to train
the AI.

You could be using code that is in a gray zone.

I would not recommend its use unless you can have an AI
that certifies its training sources.

Until the gray zone is resolved,
companies are better off not using these
AI tools for code generation or creating their own.

The ideal is to be wise and use AIs as a tool,
but know when it is appropriate and when not.

Not to do it blindly.

It is fine to use AI tools to learn and experiment.
They are something we must use if the industry demands it, but we must be aware
of the small details. Over time AIs will be very powerful;
I see the industry in 30 more years being able to generate entities that pass the
Turing test and the uncanny valley being almost nonexistent.

I only hope that the tools are used for the benefit
of humanity and the planet in an ethical and harmonious way.

